    "starters": [
      {
        "id": "restricted-line-inverse",
        "title": "Reverse a restricted decreasing line",
        "marks": 5,
        "question": "<p>Let \\(f(x)=7-2x\\) for \\(-1\\le x&lt;5\\).</p><ol class=\"parts\"><li>Find the range of \\(f\\). <strong>[2]</strong></li><li>Find \\(f^{-1}(x)\\) and state its domain. <strong>[2]</strong></li><li>Find \\(f^{-1}(-1)\\). <strong>[1]</strong></li></ol>",
        "solution": "<ol class=\"parts\"><li>The included end gives \\(f(-1)=9\\). The excluded end would give \\(f(5)=-3\\). The function decreases throughout, so the range is \\((-3,9]\\). <strong>[2]</strong></li><li>Rearranging \\(y=7-2x\\) gives \\(x=(7-y)/2\\). Thus \\(f^{-1}(x)=(7-x)/2\\) on \\((-3,9]\\). <strong>[2]</strong></li><li>\\(f^{-1}(-1)=(7+1)/2=4\\). <strong>[1]</strong></li></ol>"
      },
      {
        "id": "inverse-with-a-missing-output",
        "title": "Reverse a fractional rule",
        "marks": 6,
        "question": "<p>Let \\(g(x)=2+\\dfrac5{x+1}\\) for \\(x&gt;-1\\).</p><ol class=\"parts\"><li>Find the range of \\(g\\). <strong>[2]</strong></li><li>Find \\(g^{-1}(x)\\) and state its domain. <strong>[3]</strong></li><li>Find the input \\(x\\) for which \\(g^{-1}(x)=4\\). <strong>[1]</strong></li></ol>",
        "solution": "<ol class=\"parts\"><li>As \\(x+1\\) runs through all positive numbers, \\(5/(x+1)\\) does too. The range is \\((2,\\infty)\\). <strong>[2]</strong></li><li>From \\((y-2)(x+1)=5\\), obtain \\(g^{-1}(x)=-1+5/(x-2)\\) with \\(x&gt;2\\). Its domain is the original range. <strong>[3]</strong></li><li>Apply \\(g\\) to \\(4\\). The required input is \\(g(4)=2+5/5=3\\). <strong>[1]</strong></li></ol>"
      }
    ],
